  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 119 PM CDT Mon Oct 12 2026\n\nSouthwest winds, mostly clear skies, and above normal temperatures \nare expected through tomorrow with highs in the low 90s this \nafternoon and lows tonight ranging from around 60 degrees across \nwest central Kansas to low 70s across south central Kansas. The only \nexception to this will be along the I-70 corridor where a frontal \nboundary will move in from the north tonight with increased cloud \ncover by tomorrow morning. This frontal boundary will ooze slowly \nsouthward tomorrow. Winds behind this frontal boundary will shift to \nthe east to northeast. Highs tomorrow look to range from the upper \n70s along the I-70 corridor with upper 80s to low 90s elsewhere. \n\nAttention then pivots to tomorrow afternoon when strong to severe \nthunderstorms are expected to initially form east of Highway 283. \nThese storms then could backbuild farther west tomorrow evening into \nthe overnight period. SPC currently has a slight risk of severe \nstorms east of a line from Liberal to Dodge City, to Russell. Storms \nin this area will have the potential to produce large hail and \ndamaging winds. An isolated tornado will also be possible across \nsouth central Kansas shortly after storm initiation. Precipitation \nchances remain high east of Highway 83 Tuesday night spreading \nacross the remainder of the CWA Wednesday. The best chance of \nprecipitation looks to be Wednesday night as another disturbance \nmoves through the area. Precipitation chances then come to an end \nThursday.  The remainder of the forecast is expected to remain dry \nas upper level ridging builds across the western United States with \nhigh pressure becoming the dominate feature across the Central \nPlains. Cooler weather is also expected Wednesday through the end of \nthe forecast with highs in the 60s to low 70s Wednesday and \nThursday, then upper 50s to around 60 degrees Friday. We then \nrebound into the 60s Saturday with 70s Sunday and Monday.",
